Certainly! Here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(" ");
  const adjectives = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    if (words[i].match(/^(\w+)\s+(\w+)$/)) {
      adjectives.push(words[i]);
    }
  }

  if (adjectives.length > 1) {
    const firstAdjective = adjectives.shift();
    const lastAdjective = adjectives.pop();

    adjectives.unshift(lastAdjective);
    adjectives.push(firstAdjective);

    const newSentence = adjectives.join(" ");
    return newSentence;
  } else {
    return sentence;
  }
}

const inputSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const outputSentence = switchAdjectives(inputSentence);
console.log(outputSentence); // Output: Green beautiful trees are in the park
```

This function splits the input sentence into an array of words, and then iterates through the array looking for adjective pairs (e.g., "beautiful green"). If it finds any, it adds them to a new array called `adjectives`.

If there are more than one adjective pair, it removes the first and last adjectives from the `adjectives` array, reverses their order, and then puts them back into the array. Finally, it joins the adjectives back together into a new sentence and returns it.

If there are no adjective pairs in the input sentence, it simply returns the input sentence unchanged.

