To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in your Next.js application when using WordPress, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the `cors` package:

First, make sure you have the `cors` package installed in your Next.js project. You can install it using npm or yarn:

```bash
npm install --save cors
```

or

```bash
yarn add cors
```

2. Configure the `cors` middleware:

Next, configure the `cors` middleware in your Next.js application. Add the following code to your `pages/api/cors.js` file:

```javascript
import { Cors } from 'cors';

export default Cors({
  methods: ['GET', 'HEAD'],
  origin: 'https://your-wordpress-site.com', // Replace with the URL of your WordPress site
});
```

Replace `https://your-wordpress-site.com` with the actual URL of your WordPress site. This will allow requests from your WordPress site to your Next.js application.

3. Use the `cors` middleware in your API routes:

In your Next.js API routes, you can use the `cors` middleware to handle CORS requests. For example, if you have an API route that fetches data from your WordPress site, you can use the `cors` middleware like this:

```javascript
import { Cors } from 'cors';

export default async function handler(req, res) {
  const { method } = req;

  if (method === 'GET') {
    // Fetch data from your WordPress site
    const response = await fetch('https://your-wordpress-site.com/api/data');
    const data = await response.json();

    res.status(200).json(data);
  } else {
    res.status(405).json({ error: 'Method not allowed' });
  }
}
```

Replace `https://your-wordpress-site.com/api/data` with the actual URL of your WordPress site's API endpoint.

